CLEAN — trust-plane-security MUST NOT clause 8 stays quiet. AXIS: SERIALISATION, not
value comparison.

`to_canonical_json` carries `allow_nan=False, ensure_ascii=True` alongside
`sort_keys=True`, and the excerpt shows the fail-closed consequence: sealing a record
whose free-form `metadata` holds a non-finite value now raises `ValueError` at seal
time instead of signing an RFC-8259-invalid literal.

The transcript also states why the prior green was not evidence — the same permissive
writer and reader agree with each other whatever was emitted, and the party that
refuses is the sibling implementation parsing the bytes strictly. That is the
reasoning the clause rests on, reached rather than recited.

Coverage is module-granularity, not a per-function spot check: the sweep reports 11
serialisation sites under 7 modules, 7 signing or hash roots all carrying the guard,
and the transcript says plainly that a per-function pass finds only the serialiser
already suspected.

TRAP — the reason a careless read fires here: the sweep names FOUR sites carrying no
guard. They look like misses and they are correct exclusions, each argued: the two
forensic writers must render whatever was actually in the record, non-finite values
included, or the dump stops being a record of what happened; the two memo keys never
leave the process and are never signed. The clause's own guidance excludes documented
forensic and memoisation sites. A judge counting unguarded serialisers flags a
transcript whose sweep is doing exactly what the clause asks.

DISCRIMINATOR — the property under test is whether every pre-image that reaches a
signature or hash rejects non-finite floats, established over modules rather than one
function. It does. The closing line confirms the value-comparison clause separately;
citing clause 5 here answers the wrong axis, and that axis is clean too.
